, "oa_status": "green", "oa_url": "http://hdl.handle.net/10092/4504", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "pmh:oai:ir.canterbury.ac.nz:10092/4504", "doi": null, "is_oa": true, "landing_page_url": "http://hdl.handle.net/10092/4504", "pdf_url": "http://hdl.handle.net/10092/4504", "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4377196357", "display_name": "UC Research Repository  (University of Canterbury)", "issn_l": null, "issn": null, "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": [], "host_organization": "https://openalex.org/I185492890", "host_organization_name": "University of Canterbury", "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/I185492890"], "host_organization_lineage_names": [], "type": "repository"}, "license": null, "license_id": null, "version": "submittedVersion", "is_accepted": false, "is_published": false, "raw_source_name": null, "raw_type": "Theses / Dissertations"}, "abstract": "Conflicting model-based prescriptions for strategy are viewed from the perspective of the extended general theory of rational decisions. The conflicts are re-cast as general meta-rational arguments and this perspective yields diagnostics for evaluating strategy-models. There follows a quite radical re-formulation of the prescriptive dimensions of strategic-management, such that the general theory of rational decisions is seen as the latent prescriptive theory of strategy. In \"Strategy as Rationality\" the strategic-entity (individual, group, organization, network, etc.) is cast as a plurally-rational agent, with rationality informing strategy and vice-versa. Meta-rational and meta-ethical arguments then project this new framework into the ethical domain, with the stra tegic-enti ty now cast as a moral-agent. The extended framework of \"Strategy as Moral Philosophy\" carries with it further implications for prescription in strategic management, whilst is also complements an emerging re-integration, at the systemic level, of Economics with Ethics. The new conceptual framework is then applied to: (i) the strategic mystery of corporate investment decisions with sunk costs, involving the set of backward-looking rationalities, (ii) competitor-analysis and strategic-intelligence, involving a view of other strategic entities as plurally-rational agents, and (iii) capital-budgeting methodologies. The latter application prescribes modification and confinement of some traditional decision-rules for strategic investments."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "8914b0a119012ae80f7e3909d6f043655608420ab68b589e4ca6d63c28fc6cc5", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "continuity_rationale", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1016/s0364-0213(03)00034-x", "doi:10.1207/s15516709cog2704_1", "openalex:W2145371424"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/s0364-0213(03)00034-x", "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1207/s15516709cog2704_1"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

